***GDPR***
Against the GDPR , what is commendable is the  swiftness adhering to Art 33(1) In the case 
of a personal data breach, the controller shall without undue delay and, where feasible, not
later than 72 hours after having become aware of it, notify the personal data breach to the 
supervisory authority competent. Where I identified laxity is that the walls of  governance 
had failed to contain the disaster  before the breach.
MAG has disclosed the breach and its notification timeline, but has not disclosed how consent
was originally obtained for Wi-Fi sign-up data collection, nor whether a withdrawal mechanism 
existed and was genuinely accessible. Under Article 7(1), the burden of proving valid consent
rests with the controller — this is not something a data subject or regulator has to disprove.
Until MAG produces that evidence, Article 7 compliance cannot be assumed, and the multi-year 
retention of that same data (Article 5(1)(c), 5(1)(e)) suggests the original purpose limitation 
was not being actively enforced regardless of how consent was initially framed.






